==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|Castile|-ian}} as a [[calque]] of {{der|en|es|castellano||Of or [[relate]]d to [[Castile]], [[Spain]], or the [[Spanish]] [[language]]}}. {{doublet|en|castellano|castellanus|castellan|chatelain}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈstɪljən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-Castilian.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# The [[#Adjective|Castilian]] [[dialect]] of [[Spanish]], [[often]] {{lb|en|especially|_|historically}} [[considered]] the [[prestige dialect]] of [[Spanish]].
#: {{syn|en|Castilian Spanish}}
# {{lb|en|inexact|sometimes|_|offensive}} {{synonym of|en|Spanish}}, the [[language]] of [[Spain]] and its [[former]] [[colonies]].

====Usage notes====
Modern English use of ''Castilian'' to mean {{m|en|Spanish}} generally calques that of various Spanish dialects, whose acceptance of {{m|es|castellano}} in that sense varies widely. It is especially common in Argentinian and Peruvian Spanish and particularly uncommon in Mexican Spanish. It can also be intended to denote proper formal Spanish as opposed to slang, informal, or regional Spanish.

